### Panda Pocket - Merchant service, accounts and authentication
###
### Two credential types, deliberately not interchangeable:
###
###   API key  a merchant's SERVER calling the invoice API. Long lived, because
###            a server cannot retype a password. Sent as X-API-Key.
###   JWT      a PERSON signing in to the dashboard. Short lived, because a
###            browser session should not outlive the human. Sent as Bearer.
###
### Letting an API key manage API keys would mean a leaked key could mint
### replacements for itself and revoke the real ones, so key management is
### behind the JWT and only the JWT.

@gw = http://localhost:5000
@merchant = http://localhost:5001

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### The 401 demonstration. This is the day 4 acceptance test.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### No key at all. 401 from the GATEWAY: the request never reaches Invoice.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{ "amountZar": 250, "reference": "NO-KEY-001", "asset": "BTC" }

### A key that does not exist. Also 401, with the same message. The response
### never distinguishes "no such key" from "revoked key", because doing so would
### tell an attacker which of their guesses had once been real.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json
X-API-Key: pk_live_thiskeydoesnotexistatall000000000000000

{ "amountZar": 250, "reference": "BAD-KEY-001", "asset": "BTC" }

### The seeded demo key. 201.
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json
X-API-Key: pk_live_demo0000000000000000000000000000000000

{ "amountZar": 250, "reference": "GOOD-KEY-001", "asset": "BTC" }

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Header forgery, which is the attack this design exists to stop.
###
### X-Merchant-Id is how Invoice knows who is calling. If a client could set it,
### a valid key for merchant A would let you create invoices billed to merchant
### B, and then read B's invoices back.
###
### The gateway strips this header from every inbound request BEFORE
### authenticating, then sets it itself from the validated key. So the header
### below is discarded and the invoice belongs to the demo merchant, not to the
### GUID asserted here. Compare the merchantId in the response.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{gw}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json
X-API-Key: pk_live_demo0000000000000000000000000000000000
X-Merchant-Id: 99999999-9999-9999-9999-999999999999

{ "amountZar": 99, "reference": "FORGED-HEADER-001", "asset": "BTC" }

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Registration and dashboard login
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Sign-up is necessarily anonymous: there is no account to authenticate against
### yet. 201.
POST {{gw}}/api/merchants
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "businessName": "Second Coffee Shop",
  "email": "owner@secondcoffee.co.za",
  "password": "another-password-456",
  "feePercent": 1.5
}

### Log in. Returns a JWT carrying the merchant id as a signed claim, which is
### what makes it unforgeable: change the claim and the signature no longer
### verifies.
# @name login
POST {{gw}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "owner@democoffee.co.za",
  "password": "demo-password-123"
}

### Wrong password. 401, and the same message as an unknown email.
### The service also verifies the password against a dummy hash when no user is
### found, so an unknown email takes the same time as a known one. Returning
### early would make login an oracle for enumerating valid accounts by timing.
POST {{gw}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "owner@democoffee.co.za",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### API key management, JWT protected
###
### Paste the token from the login response above.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@token = PASTE_TOKEN_HERE
@merchantId = 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111

### Issue a key. The plaintext appears in this response and NOWHERE ELSE, ever.
### Only a SHA-256 hash is stored, so a dump of the api_keys table yields nothing
### usable. That is also why the "show me my key again" button every merchant
### asks for cannot exist: losing a key means issuing a new one.
POST {{gw}}/api/merchants/{{merchantId}}/api-keys
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{ "label": "production server" }

### List keys. Prefixes only. There is no code path anywhere that returns a key.
GET {{gw}}/api/merchants/{{merchantId}}/api-keys
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Another merchant's record. 403, not 404.
### The resource exists and the caller is authenticated; what they lack is
### authorisation, which is exactly what Forbidden means. The merchant id is read
### from the signed token, never from the URL, so changing the GUID below cannot
### grant access.
GET {{gw}}/api/merchants/99999999-9999-9999-9999-999999999999
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### No token at all. 401.
GET {{gw}}/api/merchants/{{merchantId}}/api-keys

### Revoke a key. 204. Replace the id with one from the list above.
### The row is marked, not deleted: "which key signed this transaction" stays
### answerable after the key is retired.
DELETE {{gw}}/api/api-keys/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Changing the webhook URL raises MERCHANT_WEBHOOK_URL_CHANGED in Seq.
### That event is in the catalogue on purpose: repointing where payment
### notifications are delivered is a classic account takeover step, because the
### real merchant stops hearing about payments and the attacker starts.
PUT {{gw}}/api/merchants/{{merchantId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

{ "webhookUrl": "https://example.com/new-webhook-endpoint" }

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Internal
###
### This endpoint is NOT routed through Ocelot, so it is unreachable from
### outside the compose network. A public key-validation endpoint would be an
### oracle letting anyone test guessed keys at will. Reachable here only because
### 5001 is published for development.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{merchant}}/api/internal/keys/validate
Content-Type: application/json

{ "apiKey": "pk_live_demo0000000000000000000000000000000000" }

### Proof it is not exposed: the same call through the gateway is rejected by the
### API key middleware before routing is even considered, so it returns 401. Even
### with a valid key it would then fail to match any Ocelot route. Two
### independent reasons it cannot be reached from outside.
POST {{gw}}/api/internal/keys/validate
Content-Type: application/json

{ "apiKey": "pk_live_demo0000000000000000000000000000000000" }

### Health, and the OpenAPI document.
GET {{merchant}}/health

###
GET {{merchant}}/swagger/v1/swagger.json
